WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.412 --> 00:00:25.420
For days our ship had been tossed at sea.

2
00:00:26.201 --> 00:00:28.061
The broken masts had gone overboard.

3
00:00:28.942 --> 00:00:33.984
Leaks had sprung everywhere, and the water was rising rapidly as the raging storm grew stronger.

4
00:00:34.772 --> 00:00:39.034
My wife, Elizabeth, gathered our four young sons around her in our cabin.

5
00:00:39.814 --> 00:00:42.435
She was encouraging them with calm and loving words.

6
00:00:45.037 --> 00:00:48.698
This was not the scene we had pictured when we set out from Switzerland to establish a new

7
00:00:48.738 --> 00:00:55.161
colony. Amid the roar of the thundering waves, I suddenly heard a seaman cry, "Land!

8
00:00:55.621 --> 00:01:01.444
Land!" At the same instant, the ship struck something with a jolt that threw everyone to the deck

9
00:01:01.484 --> 00:01:02.004
of the cabin.

10
00:01:03.132 --> 00:01:08.715
Above the dreadful sounds of the ship breaking up, I heard the captain shout, "Lower the lifeboats!

11
00:01:09.056 --> 00:01:09.936
Abandon ship!"

12
00:01:09.956 --> 00:01:11.437
"Abandon ship!"

13
00:01:11.757 --> 00:01:12.378
I exclaimed.

14
00:01:13.158 --> 00:01:15.299
The words went like a dagger through my heart.

15
00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:18.941
But seeing my children's terror, I hid my own fear.

16
00:01:18.961 --> 00:01:20.882
"Take courage, my boys!"

17
00:01:21.223 --> 00:01:21.643
I cried.

18
00:01:21.663 --> 00:01:24.164
"We're all still above water.

19
00:01:24.485 --> 00:01:25.585
There is land nearby.

20
00:01:26.246 --> 00:01:27.606
Let's do our best to reach it."

21
00:01:28.707 --> 00:01:30.188
With that I went up on deck.

22
00:01:31.164 --> 00:01:35.408
Imagine my horror when I saw all the lifeboats out on the water.

23
00:01:35.428 --> 00:01:36.669
The crew had abandoned us!

24
00:01:37.629 --> 00:01:42.013
But as I looked around in despair, I realized that our situation was not hopeless.

25
00:01:43.634 --> 00:01:48.258
Although the bow of the ship had been destroyed, our cabin remained unbroken in the stern.

26
00:01:49.138 --> 00:01:51.340
I could just make out some land through the rain.

27
00:01:52.601 --> 00:01:54.663
I returned to my family and forced a smile.

28
00:01:54.683 --> 00:01:58.466
"If the wind and waves die down tomorrow, we can go ashore."

29
00:01:59.367 --> 00:02:01.728
"Then we must find food," said my wife.

30
00:02:01.748 --> 00:02:04.529
"We'll need all our strength tomorrow."

31
00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:14.372
After a good supper, the three youngest boys—Ernest, 13; Jack, 11; and Franz, 8—were soon asleep.

32
00:02:15.052 --> 00:02:19.253
But at 15, Fritz was old enough to understand the danger and to stay up with us.

33
00:02:19.273 --> 00:02:22.514
"Father, you and I can swim," he said.

34
00:02:22.534 --> 00:02:26.555
"But don't you think we should make life preservers for Mother and the boys?"

35
00:02:26.575 --> 00:02:28.736
"An excellent idea!"

36
00:02:28.916 --> 00:02:29.556
I told Fritz.

37
00:02:30.336 --> 00:02:33.757
"Let's make some right now in case we need to escape the ship during the night."

38
00:02:34.438 --> 00:02:38.359
We searched the ship and soon got hold of enough empty flasks and canisters.

39
00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:42.921
Connecting them together, we made life preservers, which we all quickly put on.

40
00:02:47.923 --> 00:02:51.805
Fritz and his brothers now slept soundly while Elizabeth and I kept watch all night.

41
00:02:55.206 --> 00:02:57.787
The next morning we gathered on the remaining portion of the deck.

42
00:02:58.647 --> 00:02:59.928
"Where are the sailors?"

43
00:03:00.392 --> 00:03:01.833
asked my youngest son, Franz.

44
00:03:01.853 --> 00:03:04.473
"Have they taken away all the boats?"

45
00:03:04.913 --> 00:03:05.574
asked Jack.

46
00:03:06.694 --> 00:03:07.574
I nodded gravely.

47
00:03:07.594 --> 00:03:12.496
"The sea will soon be calm enough for swimming," Fritz reassured his younger brothers.

48
00:03:12.516 --> 00:03:14.776
"That's fine for you and Father!"

49
00:03:15.137 --> 00:03:15.957
exclaimed Ernest.

50
00:03:15.977 --> 00:03:18.478
"But think of Mother and the rest of us!

51
00:03:19.298 --> 00:03:21.919
Why not build a raft so we can all go ashore together?"

52
00:03:21.939 --> 00:03:26.100
"It would be difficult to build a raft that would carry us safely," I said.

53
00:03:26.860 --> 00:03:30.463
"But let's think about that while we explore the ship to see what might be of use to

54
00:03:30.563 --> 00:03:35.588
us." While I checked on the food and water, my wife took Franz to help her feed the

55
00:03:35.668 --> 00:03:36.488
animals on board.

56
00:03:37.609 --> 00:03:39.111
Jack went to the captain's cabin.

57
00:03:40.312 --> 00:03:44.055
When he opened the door, out sprang two large but friendly dogs!

58
00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:52.302
Back in our cabin we displayed our treasures.

59
00:03:53.162 --> 00:03:56.878
Fritz brought a couple of guns, powder flasks, and plenty of bullets.

60
00:03:57.859 --> 00:04:02.923
Ernest had nails, a saw, and a hammer while other tools stuck out of his pockets.

61
00:04:03.964 --> 00:04:05.725
"Look at these nice sharp hooks!"

62
00:04:06.106 --> 00:04:09.088
said little Franz, proudly showing us a box of fishhooks.

63
00:04:09.108 --> 00:04:10.990
"Well done, Franz!"

64
00:04:11.310 --> 00:04:11.610
I said.

65
00:04:11.630 --> 00:04:14.593
"Those may save our lives by keeping us fed."

66
00:04:15.374 --> 00:04:17.716
"I have some good news," said my dear wife.

67
00:04:18.536 --> 00:04:25.602
"Some useful animals are still alive: a cow, a donkey, two goats, six sheep, and a fine pig."

68
00:04:25.622 --> 00:04:29.185
"Now we just have to figure out how to get ashore," I said.

69
00:04:29.205 --> 00:04:32.987
"Can't we each get into a big tub and float there?"

70
00:04:33.187 --> 00:04:33.728
said Jack.

71
00:04:33.748 --> 00:04:36.810
"I've often sailed like that around the pond at home."

72
00:04:36.830 --> 00:04:38.531
"A splendid idea!"

73
00:04:38.771 --> 00:04:39.132
I said.

74
00:04:39.972 --> 00:04:43.214
We soon found four tall wooden casks floating in the hold.

75
00:04:45.956 --> 00:04:50.419
After dragging them on deck, we managed— with some difficulty—to saw them in half.

76
00:04:52.744 --> 00:04:56.646
I was proud of our work, but my wife looked in dismay at the eight tubs.

77
00:04:56.666 --> 00:04:59.268
"I'll never get into one of those!"

78
00:04:59.648 --> 00:05:00.249
she declared.

79
00:05:00.269 --> 00:05:02.110
"Just wait," I said.

80
00:05:03.230 --> 00:05:07.593
I arranged my tubs in a row, connecting them with planks along the bottom and sides.

81
00:05:08.453 --> 00:05:12.676
When I finished, I was confident our boat would be perfectly safe in smooth water.

82
00:05:14.977 --> 00:05:19.280
There was only one problem: Our boat was so heavy that we couldn't move it an inch.
